### Polars version checks

- [X] I have checked that this issue has not already been reported.

- [X] I have confirmed this bug exists on the [latest version](https://crates.io/crates/polars) of Polars.


### Issue description


## Polars incorrectly parses nanosecond datetimes when provided a schema.  
`2023-05-14T22:00:25.935632809Z` gets parsed to `1970-01-20 11:48:21.625935632 UT`

## Polars truncates nanosecond datetimes to microsecond when inferring the schema.
Polars otherwise correctly parses the timestamp and timezone, just with the time truncated.

## `strptime` "forgets" about the timezone when using a tz-aware format string
 


### Reproducible examples

A common prelude to each test
```rust
use polars::prelude::{CsvReader, DataType, TimeUnit, Field, Schema};
const TEXT: &str = r#"trial,start,end,status,count
trial-1,2023-05-14T22:00:25.935632809Z,,Active,19227
trial-2,2023-05-14T22:00:25.932416044Z,2023-05-19T21:00:00.182534337Z,Active,4346
trial-3,2023-05-15T01:07:27.572695628Z,2023-05-19T20:26:47.402457930Z,Active,3045
"#;
```

## Polars incorrectly parses nanosecond datetimes when provided a schema.  
```rust
/// Polars incorrectly parses nanoseconds timestamp when given a schema
#[test]
fn test_incorrectly_parses_with_nanosecond_schema() {
    let fields = vec![
        Field::new("trial", DataType::Utf8),
        Field::new(
            "start",
            DataType::Datetime(TimeUnit::Nanoseconds, Some("UTC".to_owned())),
        ),
        Field::new("end", DataType::Datetime(TimeUnit::Nanoseconds, None)),
        Field::new("status", DataType::Utf8),
        Field::new("count", DataType::Int64),
    ];
    let schema = Schema::from_iter(fields);


    let cursor = std::io::Cursor::new(TEXT);
    let df = CsvReader::new(cursor)
        .has_header(true)
        .with_dtypes(Some(schema.into()))
        .finish()
        .unwrap();
    println!("{:?}", df);

    let started_2023 = df.lazy()
        .filter(col("start").dt().year().eq(2023))
        .collect().unwrap();
    // fails with 0
    assert_eq!(started_2023.height(), 3);
}
```

