2. 

It certainly might be the intended behavior. Are there other examples where `polars` defaults to silently truncating or otherwise discarding data?

3.

Sorry about the typo in the example, the issue reproduces without it. I have refined the example to make it more explicit.

> 1. When `exact: true`, the timezone is properly parsed and added to the resulting dtype. This matches my expected behavior.
> 2. When `exact: false`, the timezone is forgotten. 
> 
> (Conditional)
> 3. If `exact: false` is intended to forget the timezone, then I'd expect the ability to explicitly set it in the dtype.


 Here is a clearer example
 ```rust
 fn parse_nanos_ts(input_col: &str, exact: bool, datatype_tz: Option<String>) -> polars::prelude::Expr {
    let strptime_options = polars::prelude::StrptimeOptions {
        // exact will only parse complete strings
        exact,
        // the format string needs the .9f for nanoseconds
        // the %#z makes the format string `tz-aware`, but polars fails to *include* that in the final type
        format: Some("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%.9f%#z".to_string()),
        // error out if parsing fails
        strict: true,
        // do not build up a cache of nanoseconds timestamps
        cache: false,
    };

    // the timezone on dt_type must be `None` if using a dt_aware formatstring (i.e. the `%#z`)
    let dt_type_no_tz = DataType::Datetime(polars::prelude::TimeUnit::Nanoseconds, datatype_tz);

    let out_col = format!("{}-{}", input_col, exact);
    col(input_col)
        .str()
        .strptime(dt_type_no_tz, strptime_options)
        .alias(&out_col)
}

#[test]
fn test_strptime_forgets_tz_when_exact_is_false() {
    let cursor = std::io::Cursor::new(TEXT);
    let df = CsvReader::new(cursor)
        .has_header(true)
        .with_try_parse_dates(false)
        .finish()
        .unwrap();


    let df = df
        .lazy()
        .with_columns([
            parse_nanos_ts("start", true, None), // this will remember the timezone
            parse_nanos_ts("start", false, None), // this will forget the timezone
        ])
        .collect()
        .unwrap();
    println!("{:?}", df);

    let tz = "UTC";
    let dt_type_with_tz = DataType::Datetime(polars::prelude::TimeUnit::Nanoseconds, Some(tz.to_string()));

    let expected = &dt_type_with_tz;
    
    // when parsing with `exact: true`, polars remembers the timezone correctly
    let received_exact = df.column("start-true").unwrap().dtype();
    assert_eq!(expected, received_exact, "parsing-with-exact"); // this succeeds

    // when parsing with `exact: false`, polars forgets the timezone
    let recieved_inexact = df.column("start-false").unwrap().dtype();
    assert_eq!(expected, recieved_inexact, "parsing-with-exact-false"); // this fails

    // It may be that the `exact: false` is the intended behavior,
    // but in that case we should be able to set an explicit dtype 
    let res = df.lazy().with_columns([
        parse_nanos_ts("start", false, Some(tz.to_string()))
    ]).collect();
    println!("res: {:?}", res);

    // "cannot use strptime with both a tz-aware format and a tz-aware dtype, please drop time zone from the dtype"
    assert!(res.is_ok(), "parsing-with-explicit-tz-failed");
}
